---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import FeatureList from '../components/FeatureList.astro';
import { EDGE_FEATURES, STATUS, product } from '../lib/products';

const p = product('edge');
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop Edge: HMI, PLCs and store-and-forward"
  description="HotLoop Edge is HotLoop IoT plus the machine layer: HMI screens, PLCs, store-and-forward and an OPC UA server. It arrives in an upcoming release."
  ogAlt="HotLoop Edge, IoT plus the machine layer."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: p.name,
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/edge/',
      description:
        'HotLoop IoT plus the machine layer: HMI screens, PLC connectivity, store-and-forward over Sparkplug B, and an OPC UA server, for a box that sits next to a machine.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop Edge</span><span class="chip warn">Upcoming release</span></div>
      <h1>IoT, plus everything that lives next to a machine.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Ignition Edge had the right idea, and this is ours. Everything in <a href="/iot/">HotLoop IoT</a>, plus HMI
        screens for the touch panel on the machine, PLCs, CODESYS and Pi PLCs, store-and-forward to whatever sits
        upstream, and an OPC UA server. The upshot: the machine keeps running its screens and its logic when the
        network doesn't, and the SCADA you already paid for can still read it.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>HotLoop Edge is not out yet. It comes in an upcoming release, alongside IoT.</strong>
          <p>
            No Edge image or chart today, and no date. If you need it now, run the <a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a>,
            which already has the store-and-forward and the OPC UA server Edge will carry. If all the box has to do is
            poll and forward, run the <a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/gateway/#install">Install the Gateway today</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/products/">Compare the lineup</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">What it is for</span>
        <h2>A box at the machine that keeps working when the network doesn't.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          The operator's screen runs against the box on the machine, not a server three buildings away. When the
          uplink drops, readings buffer to disk and replay in order, flagged historical, when it comes back. The
          alarm still raises, the rule still runs, the panel still answers. A backhoe through the fiber is an IT
          ticket, not a line down.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">The database</span>
        <h2>{p.databases}.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Picked by a setting, same image. TimescaleDB when the box keeps real history, SQLite when it's a small PC
          with a small disk. Moving up to the Gateway is designed to keep your data and your config, so the box you
          start with isn't a dead end.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What Edge adds to IoT</span>
        <h2>The machine layer, and where each piece stands.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          "{STATUS.released.label}" means it runs in the published Gateway today. "{STATUS.unverified.label}" means the
          path exists and nobody has pointed it at the real thing yet, so your first one is a test, not an install.
          "{STATUS.planned.label}" means exactly that. Everything in <a href="/iot/">IoT</a> comes along too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <FeatureList features={EDGE_FEATURES} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">HMI, the plan</span>
        <h2>One UI, not two.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          HMI screens aren't a second front end bolted on the side. They're a layout and a symbol library on top of
          the dashboards IoT gets, same bindings, same write gate. Two UIs means two sets of bugs and two places to
          forget a permission check, and we're not signing up for that.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>A canvas that never reflows</h3><p>A fixed screen that scales and never shuffles things around, because an operator's thumb goes to where the pump was yesterday, and it better still be there.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>ISA-101 by default</h3><p>Gray backgrounds, color only for abnormal, alarm priority by shape as well as color. When the whole screen is gray, the one red thing gets seen. No 3D gradients on the tank.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Faceplates</h3><p>Click a motor and get its popup: start, stop, reset, mode, speed, its interlocks and its alarms, built from the motor entity that already exists.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Panel mode</h3><p>Fullscreen on the touch panel on the machine, a persistent alarm banner, and big touch targets. It works against the box itself with the uplink down.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>A person behind every write</h3><p>On your own computer you're already signed in. A shared panel signs operators in with a PIN or a badge and signs them out when idle, so the audit names a human, not "panel 3".</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Nothing built yet</h3><p>All of this is design. It lands after dashboards, which it is built on, and this page will say when it moves.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free, with no license key, when it ships.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            Edge is under the HotLoop Community License, like the rest of the lineup. Individuals use it free.
            Businesses sign up for <a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNET</a> free and use it there free. Edge isn't a
            paid tier of anything, because there is no paid tier to be one of.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the terms</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
